  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Does taking complements first and then taking the intersection always give the same result as taking the intersection first and then taking the complement?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "They are always the same",
    "B": "They differ only for the empty set",
    "C": "They differ only for infinite sets",
    "D": "They are not necessarily the same"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Use the same universal set \\(U\\) for both expressions: \\(A^c\\cap B^c\\) and \\((A\\cap B)^c\\). A single counterexample is enough to show that they do not always agree.\n\nLet \\(U=\\{1,2\\}\\), \\(A=\\{1\\}\\), and \\(B=\\{2\\}\\). Then \\(A^c=\\{2\\}\\) and \\(B^c=\\{1\\}\\), so\n\\[\nA^c\\cap B^c=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nBut \\(A\\cap B=\\varnothing\\), so\n\\[\n(A\\cap B)^c=U=\\{1,2\\}.\n\\]\nThe results differ, so they are not necessarily the same. The answer is D.",
